# Heaven Web Framework - LLM Reference

Heaven is an async Python web framework built on ASGI. It provides routing, middleware (BEFORE/AFTER hooks), schema validation via pytastic, subdomain management, WebSocket support, CORS, sessions, templating, file serving, streaming, and a built-in test client (Earth).

## 1. Installation & Startup

```python
# main.py
from heaven import App

app = App()

# String discovery (preferred) - resolves to handlers.home.hello
app.GET("/", "handlers.home.hello")

# Run with uvicorn:
# uvicorn main:app --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000
# Or use:
app.listen(host="localhost", port="8701")
```

```python
# handlers/home.py
async def hello(req, res, ctx):
    res.body = "Hello, World!"
    res.status = 200
```

App is an alias for Router. Application and Server are also aliases. All are interchangeable.

```python
from heaven import App, Application, Router, Server
# App == Application == Router == Server
```

### App Constructor

```python
app = App(
    protect_output=True,     # Strip extra fields from responses by default
    allow_partials=False,    # Allow partial responses (missing required fields) by default
    fail_on_output=True,     # Return 500 on output validation failure (vs silent fallback)
    debug=True,              # Enable debug mode (Guardian Angel error pages)
    monitor=None,            # Event loop lag monitoring interval (seconds), None to disable
)
```

## 2. Handler Registration

Heaven's preferred way to register handlers is via **string discovery**. The string `"module.path.function_name"` is resolved at registration time via Python's import system:

```python
# Resolves to: from handlers.users import list_users
app.GET("/users", "handlers.users.list_users")

# Resolves to: from api.v1.orders import create_order
app.POST("/orders", "api.v1.orders.create_order")
```

The string is split at the last `.` — everything before is the module path, the last segment is the function name.

You can also pass functions directly when co-locating handlers:

```python
async def health(req, res, ctx):
    res.body = "OK"

app.GET("/health", health)
```

### Handler Signature

Every handler receives exactly 3 arguments:

```python
from heaven import Request, Response, Context

async def handler(req: Request, res: Response, ctx: Context):
    res.body = "OK"
    res.status = 200
```

Handlers can be sync or async. Heaven supports both.

### Type-Safe Handlers with Generics

`Request` is `Generic[T]`, so `req.data` can be typed via annotation. The generic parameter should match the `expects` schema:

```python
from heaven import Request, Response, Context

class User(Schema):
    name: str
    email: str

app.schema.POST("/users", expects=User)

async def create_user(req: Request[User], res: Response, ctx: Context):
    user = req.data  # IDE infers this as User, autocomplete works
    print(user.name)

app.POST("/users", create_user)
```

This is a convention — Heaven doesn't enforce the match at runtime, but type checkers (mypy, pyright) will.

## 3. Routing

### HTTP Methods

```python
app.GET("/users", "handlers.users.list_users")
app.POST("/users", "handlers.users.create_user")
app.PUT("/users/:id", "handlers.users.update_user")
app.PATCH("/users/:id", "handlers.users.patch_user")
app.DELETE("/users/:id", "handlers.users.delete_user")
app.OPTIONS("/users", "handlers.users.options")
app.CONNECT("/users", "handlers.users.connect")
app.TRACE("/users", "handlers.users.trace")

# Register handler for ALL HTTP methods on a route
# (CONNECT, DELETE, GET, OPTIONS, PATCH, POST, PUT, TRACE):
app.HTTP("/health", "handlers.health.check")
```

### Route Parameters

Use `:param` syntax. Parameters are accessible via `req.params`:

```python
app.GET("/users/:id/posts/:post_id", "handlers.posts.get_post")
```

```python
# handlers/posts.py
async def get_post(req, res, ctx):
    user_id = req.params.get("id")      # string
    post_id = req.params.get("post_id")  # string
    res.body = f"User {user_id}, Post {post_id}"
```

#### Typed Route Parameters

Parameters can be type-coerced using `:param:type` syntax. Supported types: `int`, `str`.

```python
app.GET("/users/:id:int/posts/:post_id:int", "handlers.posts.get_post")
```

```python
# handlers/posts.py
async def get_post(req, res, ctx):
    user_id = req.params.get("id")      # int (auto-coerced)
    post_id = req.params.get("post_id")  # int (auto-coerced)
```

### Wildcard Routes

Use `*` to match any remaining path. The matched portion is in `req.params.get("*")`:

```python
app.GET("/files/*", "handlers.files.serve")
```

```python
# handlers/files.py
async def serve(req, res, ctx):
    filepath = req.params.get("*")  # e.g. "docs/readme.md"
```

### Query String Hints (Queryhints)

Heaven can auto-coerce query string values to Python types. Queryhints are defined as part of the route using `?key:type&key:type` syntax:

Supported types: `int`, `str`, `float`, `datetime`, `date`, `uuid`, `bool`

```python
app.GET("/search?page:int&active:bool", "handlers.search.search")
```

```python
# handlers/search.py
async def search(req, res, ctx):
    page = req.queries.get("page")      # int (auto-coerced)
    active = req.queries.get("active")   # bool (auto-coerced)
```

## 4. Request Object

### Properties

| Property      | Type           | Description                                  |
|---------------|----------------|----------------------------------------------|
| `req.app`     | `Router`       | The application instance                     |
| `req.body`    | `bytes`        | Raw request body                             |
| `req.json`    | `dict/list`    | Parsed JSON body (via orjson)                |
| `req.data`    | `T`            | Validated data (if schema is set, see below) |
| `req.cookies` | `dict`         | Parsed cookies from Cookie header            |
| `req.form`    | `Form`/`None`  | Parsed form data (multipart or urlencoded)   |
| `req.headers` | `dict`         | Request headers                              |
| `req.host`    | `str`          | Host header value                            |
| `req.ip`      | `Lookup`       | Client IP (`req.ip.address`, `req.ip.port`)  |
| `req.method`  | `str`          | HTTP method (`GET`, `POST`, etc.)            |
| `req.params`  | `dict`         | Route parameters                             |
| `req.queries` | `dict`         | Parsed query string parameters               |
| `req.querystring` | `bytes/str`| Raw query string                             |
| `req.route`   | `str`          | Matched route pattern                        |
| `req.scheme`  | `str`          | Request scheme (`http`, `https`)             |
| `req.server`  | `str`          | Server address (`host:port`)                 |
| `req.subdomain` | `str`        | Resolved subdomain (or `www` for default)    |
| `req.url`     | `str`          | Request path                                 |

### req.data - Automatic Validation & Deserialization

When a schema is registered for a route via `app.schema`, the request body is automatically validated and made available as `req.data`. This uses pytastic under the hood:

```python
# schemas.py
from typing import TypedDict, Annotated

class CreateUser(TypedDict):
    name: Annotated[str, "min_len=2"]
    email: Annotated[str, "format=email"]
```

```python
# main.py
from schemas import CreateUser

app.schema.POST("/users", expects=CreateUser)
app.POST("/users", "handlers.users.create_user")
```

```python
# handlers/users.py
async def create_user(req: Request[CreateUser], res: Response, ctx: Context):
    # req.data is already validated - if validation fails,
    # heaven automatically returns 422 before this handler runs
    user = req.data       # IDE knows this is CreateUser
    print(user["name"])   # guaranteed to be a string with len >= 2
    print(user["email"])  # guaranteed to be a valid email
    res.body = {"id": 1, **user}
    res.status = 201
```

If no schema is set, `req.data` falls back to `req.json`. Use `Request[T]` generics to get IDE autocomplete on `req.data` (see Handler Signature section above).

### Form Data

```python
async def upload(req, res, ctx):
    form = req.form
    username = form.get("username")           # string field
    avatar = form.get("avatar")               # File object
    if avatar:
        print(avatar.filename)                # original filename
        print(avatar.content)                 # bytes content

    # Attribute access also works:
    username = form.username

    # Get all fields as a dict:
    all_data = form.to_dict()
```

## 5. Response Object

### Setting the Response Body

```python
async def handler(req, res, ctx):
    res.body = "text"                    # str -> encoded to bytes
    res.body = 42                        # int/float -> str -> bytes
    res.body = {"key": "value"}          # dict -> auto-serialized to JSON
    res.body = [1, 2, 3]                 # list -> auto-serialized to JSON
    res.body = b"raw bytes"              # bytes -> sent as-is
```

### Setting Status

```python
res.status = 200
res.status = 404

# Or use HTTPStatus:
from http import HTTPStatus
res.status = HTTPStatus.CREATED          # 201
```

### Headers

```python
res.headers = "Content-Type", "application/json"
res.headers = "X-Custom", "value"

# Chaining with .header():
res.header("X-One", "1").header("X-Two", "2")
```

### Cookies

```python
res.cookie("session_id", "abc123",
    max_age=3600,
    httponly=True,
    secure=True,
    samesite="Lax",
    path="/",
    domain=".example.com"
)
```

Supported cookie options: `expires` (datetime), `secure` (bool), `httponly`/`http_only` (bool), `samesite`/`same_site` (str: "Strict"/"Lax"/"None"), `domain` (str), `path` (str), `partitioned` (bool), `max_age`/`maxage` (int).

### Redirect

```python
res.redirect("/login")                   # 307 Temporary Redirect
res.redirect("/new-url", permanent=True) # 308 Permanent Redirect
```

### Abort

Abort stops further processing (skips remaining AFTER hooks):

```python
async def handler(req, res, ctx):
    if not authorized:
        res.status = 403
        res.abort("Forbidden")
        return
```

### Streaming

```python
async def handler(req, res, ctx):
    async def generate():
        for i in range(10):
            yield f"chunk {i}\n".encode()

    res.stream(generate(), content_type="text/plain", status=200)
```

### Server-Sent Events (SSE)

When `sse=True`, each yielded item is wrapped in `data: ...\n\n` format. Dicts and lists are JSON-serialized automatically:

```python
async def handler(req, res, ctx):
    async def events():
        for i in range(5):
            yield f"event {i}"           # sent as: data: event 0\n\n
        yield {"count": 5}               # sent as: data: {"count":5}\n\n

    res.stream(events(), sse=True)
```

### File Serving

```python
async def download(req, res, ctx):
    res.file("/path/to/report.pdf", filename="report.pdf")
```

### Deferred Tasks

Run tasks after the response is sent. Deferred functions receive the app as their argument:

```python
async def handler(req, res, ctx):
    res.body = "Accepted"
    res.status = 202

    async def send_email(app):
        # runs after response is sent
        await email_service.send(...)

    res.defer(send_email)
```

### Templating (HTML Rendering)

```python
app.TEMPLATES("templates", relative_to=__file__)

# Async rendering (default):
async def page(req, res, ctx):
    await res.render("index.html", title="Home", items=[1, 2, 3])

# Sync rendering:
app.TEMPLATES("templates", asynchronous=False)
def page(req, res, ctx):
    res.renders("index.html", title="Home")
```

Templates use Jinja2. The template receives `ctx`, `req`, `res`, and any extra kwargs.

### String Interpolation

```python
async def handler(req, res, ctx):
    html = await res.interpolate("email_template.html", user=user_data)
    # html is a string, not set on res.body
```

### res.out() Shorthand

```python
res.out(200, {"message": "OK"})
# Equivalent to:
# res.status = 200
# res.body = {"message": "OK"}

# With optional headers:
res.out(200, {"message": "OK"}, ("X-Custom", "value"))
```

## 6. Context Object

Context is per-request scoped storage shared across handlers and middleware:

```python
async def auth_middleware(req, res, ctx):
    ctx.user = {"id": 1, "role": "admin"}     # dot notation
    ctx.keep("db_result", some_data)           # .keep() method

async def handler(req, res, ctx):
    user = ctx.user                            # dot notation
    data = ctx.peek("db_result")               # .peek() method
    ctx.unkeep("db_result")                    # remove
```

### Typed Keys

For type-safe context access:

```python
from heaven import Key

user_key = Key[dict]("current_user")

async def middleware(req, res, ctx):
    ctx.keep(user_key, {"id": 1})

async def handler(req, res, ctx):
    user = ctx.peek(user_key)  # IDE knows this is dict
```

### Reserved Keys

These keys cannot be set via dot-notation on ctx: `session`, `app`, `request`, `response`, `headers`, `cookies`.

```python
ctx.session = {"user": 1}            # raises AttributeError
ctx.keep("session", {"user": 1})     # works fine (used internally by sessions)
```

## 7. Application-Level Storage

Store data that persists across all requests:

```python
app.keep("db_pool", pool)
pool = app.peek("db_pool")
app.unkeep("db_pool")

# Also accessible via shorthand (dot-notation access via Look):
app.keep("config", {"debug": True})
print(app._.config.debug)  # True
```

Typed keys work the same way:

```python
db_key = Key[Pool]("database")
app.keep(db_key, pool)
pool = app.peek(db_key)   # IDE knows type is Pool
```

## 8. Middleware (BEFORE / AFTER Hooks)

### BEFORE Hooks

Run before the handler. Same signature as handlers:

```python
app.BEFORE("/users/*", "middleware.logging.log_request")
```

```python
# middleware/logging.py
async def log_request(req, res, ctx):
    print(f"{req.method} {req.url}")
```

You can also pass inline functions:

### AFTER Hooks

Run after the handler:

```python
async def add_headers(req, res, ctx):
    res.headers = "X-Powered-By", "Heaven"

app.AFTER("/*", add_headers)
```

### Global Middleware

Use `/*` to match all routes:

```python
app.BEFORE("/*", "middleware.auth.check")
app.AFTER("/*", "middleware.logging.log_response")
```

### Hook Execution Order

1. Global BEFORE hooks (`/*`)
2. Route-specific BEFORE hooks
3. Handler
4. Route-specific AFTER hooks
5. Global AFTER hooks (`/*`)

If a BEFORE hook calls `res.abort()`, the handler and subsequent hooks are skipped.

## 9. Lifecycle Events

### Startup / Shutdown

```python
async def on_startup(app):
    app.keep("db", await create_pool())
    print("App started")

async def on_shutdown(app):
    pool = app.peek("db")
    await pool.close()
    print("App stopped")

app.ONCE("startup", on_startup)
app.ONCE("shutdown", on_shutdown)

# String discovery works here too:
app.ONCE("startup", "lifecycle.setup.on_startup")
app.ONCE("shutdown", "lifecycle.setup.on_shutdown")

# Shorthand (defaults to startup):
app.ONCE(on_startup)
# Or:
app.ON(on_startup)
```

### Daemons

Long-running background tasks that repeat on an interval. The daemon function receives the app and returns the number of seconds to sleep before the next invocation. Return `None` or `False` to stop the daemon:

```python
async def background_worker(app):
    # do periodic work
    await check_for_updates()
    return 60  # run again in 60 seconds

app.daemons = background_worker

# Sync daemons also work (run in thread pool):
def sync_worker(app):
    cleanup_temp_files()
    return 300  # run again in 5 minutes

app.daemons = sync_worker
```

## 10. Schema Validation & API Docs

Heaven uses pytastic for schema validation. See the `.pytastic` file for full pytastic API reference.

### Schema Registration

```python
from typing import TypedDict, Annotated, List

class CreateUser(TypedDict):
    name: Annotated[str, "min_len=2; max_len=50"]
    email: Annotated[str, "format=email"]
    age: Annotated[int, "min=0; max=150"]

class UserResponse(TypedDict):
    id: int
    name: str
    email: str

# Register input and output schemas:
app.schema.POST("/users", expects=CreateUser, returns=UserResponse)
app.POST("/users", "handlers.users.create_user")

# Method-specific shortcuts:
app.schema.GET("/users", returns=List[UserResponse])
app.GET("/users", "handlers.users.list_users")

app.schema.PUT("/users/:id", expects=CreateUser, returns=UserResponse)
app.PUT("/users/:id", "handlers.users.update_user")

app.schema.DELETE("/users/:id")
app.DELETE("/users/:id", "handlers.users.delete_user")

app.schema.PATCH("/users/:id", expects=CreateUser)
app.PATCH("/users/:id", "handlers.users.patch_user")
```

### How It Works

When `expects` is defined, heaven automatically:
1. Registers a BEFORE hook that validates `req.json` against the schema
2. Stores the validated result in `req._data` (accessible via `req.data`)
3. Returns 422 with the validation error if validation fails

When `returns` is defined, heaven automatically:
1. Registers an AFTER hook that processes `res.body`
2. If `protect=True` (default), strips extra fields not in the schema using `pytastic.validate(..., strip=True)`
3. If `partial=True`, allows missing required fields using `pytastic.validate(..., partial=True)`
4. Serializes the result to JSON

### protect and partial

These control output validation behavior:

| Setting | Default | Effect |
|---------|---------|--------|
| `protect` | `True` (from `protect_output`) | Strips fields from response not defined in schema |
| `partial` | `False` (from `allow_partials`) | Allows response to omit required schema fields |
| `strict` | `True` (from `fail_on_output`) | Returns 500 on output validation error vs silent fallback |

```python
# Per-route override:
app.schema.POST("/users", returns=UserResponse, protect=True, partial=True, strict=False)

# App-level defaults:
app = App(protect_output=True, allow_partials=False, fail_on_output=True)
```

Example of protect stripping sensitive data:

```python
class PublicUser(TypedDict):
    id: int
    name: str

app.schema.GET("/users/:id", returns=PublicUser)
app.GET("/users/:id", "handlers.users.get_user")
```

```python
# handlers/users.py
async def get_user(req, res, ctx):
    # Database returns full record including password_hash
    res.body = {"id": 1, "name": "Ray", "password_hash": "abc123", "internal_note": "admin"}
    # protect=True strips password_hash and internal_note automatically
    # Client receives: {"id": 1, "name": "Ray"}
```

Example of partial allowing subset responses:

```python
class FullUser(TypedDict):
    id: int
    name: str
    email: str
    bio: str

app.schema.GET("/users", returns=List[FullUser], partial=True)
app.GET("/users", "handlers.users.list_users")
```

```python
# handlers/users.py
async def list_users(req, res, ctx):
    # Only returning summary data:
    res.body = [{"id": 1, "name": "Ray"}, {"id": 2, "name": "Jane"}]
    # partial=True: missing email & bio won't cause validation error
```

### Schema Metadata for API Docs

```python
app.schema.POST("/users",
    expects=CreateUser,
    returns=UserResponse,
    summary="Create a new user",
    description="Creates a user account with validated input",
    group="Users"          # OpenAPI tag
)
```

### OpenAPI & Interactive Docs

```python
app.DOCS("/docs", title="My API", version="1.0.0")
# Serves:
#   GET /docs          -> Scalar interactive docs UI
#   GET /docs/openapi.json -> OpenAPI 3.1.0 JSON spec
```

### General Schema Registration

```python
app.schema.add("POST", "/users",
    expects=CreateUser,
    returns=UserResponse,
    summary="Create user",
    group="Users"
)
```

## 11. CORS

```python
app.cors(
    origins=["http://localhost:3000", "https://myapp.com"],
    methods=["GET", "POST", "PUT", "DELETE"],
    headers=["Content-Type", "Authorization"],
    credentials=True,
    max_age=86400,
)
```

Or with a custom handler:

```python
async def custom_cors(req, res, ctx):
    res.headers = "Access-Control-Allow-Origin", req.headers.get("origin", "*")

app.cors(custom_cors)
```

### Subdomain CORS

By default `app.cors()` only applies to the `www` (default) subdomain. Use `subdomains` to target specific subdomains, or call `cors()` directly on a subdomain proxy:

```python
api = app.subdomain("api")
admin = app.subdomain("admin")

# Apply to specific subdomains (must be registered first):
app.cors(origins=["https://myapp.com"], subdomains=["www", "api", "admin"])

# Or call cors() on the subdomain proxy directly:
api.cors(origins=["https://myapp.com"])
```

**Note:** `cors()` automatically registers an `OPTIONS /*` catch-all route on each targeted subdomain. This ensures CORS preflight requests are handled correctly without needing to manually register OPTIONS routes.

### Custom CORS with Subdomains

A custom handler function can also be scoped to specific subdomains:

```python
async def my_cors(req, res, ctx):
    origin = req.headers.get("origin", "")
    res.headers = "Access-Control-Allow-Origin", origin
    res.headers = "Access-Control-Allow-Credentials", "true"
    res.headers = "Access-Control-Allow-Methods", "GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, OPTIONS"
    res.headers = "Access-Control-Allow-Headers", "Content-Type, Authorization"
    if req.method == "OPTIONS":
        res.status = 204
        res.body = b""
        res.abort(b"")

app.cors(my_cors, subdomains=["api"])
```

When using a custom handler, you are responsible for handling OPTIONS preflight (including `res.abort()`) yourself. The auto-registered `OPTIONS /*` route ensures the request reaches your BEFORE hook.

## 12. Sessions

Cookie-based signed sessions. The session cookie is written via `res.cookie()` internally, so all standard cookie options (domain, secure, samesite, etc.) are supported:

```python
app.sessions("my-secret-key", cookie_name="session", max_age=3600)

# With cookie options (forwarded to res.cookie()):
app.sessions("my-secret-key",
    cookie_name="session",
    max_age=3600,
    domain=".example.com",     # share session across subdomains
    secure=True,               # HTTPS only
    samesite="Strict",         # override default "Lax"
)
```

Defaults (if not overridden): `path="/"`, `httponly=True`, `samesite="Lax"`.

### Subdomain Sessions

By default `sessions()` only applies to the `www` (default) subdomain. Use `subdomains` to enable session load/save hooks on specific subdomains:

```python
api = app.subdomain("api")
my = app.subdomain("my")

# Enable sessions on www, api, and my subdomains:
app.sessions("my-secret-key",
    cookie_name="session",
    max_age=3600,
    subdomains=["www", "api", "my"],
    domain=".example.com",     # share the cookie across subdomains
)
```

The session cookie (with `domain=".example.com"`) is sent by the browser to all subdomains, but the load/save hooks only run on the subdomains you specify. This avoids unnecessary session deserialization on subdomains that don't need it (e.g., a static assets subdomain).

```python
async def login(req, res, ctx):
    ctx.session["user_id"] = 123
    res.body = "Logged in"

async def dashboard(req, res, ctx):
    user_id = ctx.session.get("user_id")
    if not user_id:
        res.abort("Not authenticated")
        res.status = 401
        return
    res.body = f"Welcome user {user_id}"
```

Sessions use `SecureSerializer` which provides HMAC-SHA256 signing with timestamps. Session data is wrapped in a `Look` object, so dot-notation access works:

```python
async def handler(req, res, ctx):
    ctx.session.user_id   # dot-notation access
    ctx.session["role"]   # dict-style access
```

## 13. Subdomain Management

```python
# Register a subdomain:
api = app.subdomain("api")
api.GET("/users", "handlers.api.users.list_users")
api.POST("/users", "handlers.api.users.create_user")
api.schema.POST("/users", expects=CreateUser)  # schema works on subdomains too

# Another subdomain:
admin = app.subdomain("admin")
admin.GET("/dashboard", "handlers.admin.dashboard.index")
admin.BEFORE("/*", "middleware.admin_auth.check")

# Wildcard subdomain (matches any subdomain):
wild = app.subdomain("*")
wild.GET("/", "handlers.wildcard.index")
```

Routes registered without a subdomain default to `www` (the default subdomain).

Heaven resolves subdomains from the `Host` header. For `api.example.com`, the subdomain is `api`. For `example.com` or IP addresses, it defaults to `www`.

### Subdomain-Scoped Features

Subdomains support all the same methods as the app:

```python
api = app.subdomain("api")
api.GET("/route", "handlers.api.route.get")
api.POST("/route", "handlers.api.route.create")
api.BEFORE("/route", "middleware.api_auth.check")
api.AFTER("/route", "middleware.api_logging.log")
api.ASSETS("static")               # Subdomain-scoped static files
api.doc("/docs")                   # Subdomain-scoped docs
api.doc("/docs", title="My API", version="1.0.0", favicon="/static/favicon.ico")
api.schema.POST("/route", expects=Schema)
```

## 14. Static Assets

```python
app.ASSETS("static", relative_to=__file__)
# Serves files from ./static/ at /static/*
# e.g. /static/css/main.css -> ./static/css/main.css

# Custom route:
app.ASSETS("public", route="/assets/*", relative_to=__file__)
```

## 15. WebSockets

```python
app.WS("/chat", "handlers.chat.ws_handler")
# Or:
app.SOCKET("/chat", "handlers.chat.ws_handler")
app.WEBSOCKET("/chat", "handlers.chat.ws_handler")
```

### WebSocket Handler Signature

WebSocket handlers have a different signature from HTTP handlers. They receive 4 arguments:

```python
async def ws_handler(sender, receiver, request, context):
    # sender(data) - async function to send data to the client
    # receiver()   - async function to receive data from the client
    # request      - Request object (has params, queries, headers, etc.)
    # context      - Context object (per-connection scoped)

    while True:
        msg = await receiver()        # receive message from client
        if msg is None:
            break
        await sender(f"Echo: {msg}")  # send message to client
```

- `sender(data)` accepts `str` (sent as text) or `bytes` (sent as binary)
- `receiver()` returns the message text or bytes, or `None` on disconnect

## 16. Mounting (Composing Apps)

```python
users_app = App()
users_app.GET("/", "handlers.users.list_users")
users_app.POST("/", "handlers.users.create_user")
users_app.ONCE("startup", "lifecycle.users.init_db")

app.mount(users_app)
# users_app routes, hooks, and lifecycle events are merged into app

# isolated=False shares buckets and configuration:
app.mount(users_app, isolated=False)
```

## 17. Plugins

```python
class MyPlugin:
    def install(self, app):
        app.BEFORE("/*", self.middleware)
        app.ONCE(self.setup)

    async def setup(self, app):
        pass

    async def middleware(self, req, res, ctx):
        pass

app.plugin(MyPlugin())
```

## 18. Configuration

Load configuration from a dict or callable:

```python
# From a dict:
app = App(configurator={"DB_URL": "postgres://localhost/mydb", "SECRET": "my-secret"})

# From a callable that returns a dict:
def load_config():
    return {"DB_URL": os.environ["DB_URL"], "SECRET": os.environ["SECRET"]}

app = App(configurator=load_config)

# Access config values:
db_url = app.CONFIG("DB_URL")
```

## 19. Security - SecureSerializer

For signing and verifying tokens/data (used internally by sessions):

```python
from heaven.security import SecureSerializer, BadSignature, SignatureExpired

signer = SecureSerializer("my-secret-key", salt="auth-tokens")

# Sign data:
token = signer.dumps({"user_id": 123, "role": "admin"})
# Returns: "base64payload.base64timestamp.base64signature"

# Verify and decode:
data = signer.loads(token, max_age=3600)
# Returns: {"user_id": 123, "role": "admin"}
# Raises SignatureExpired if token is older than max_age
# Raises BadSignature if tampered

# With schema validation:
from typing import TypedDict
class TokenPayload(TypedDict):
    user_id: int
    role: str

data = signer.loads(token, type=TokenPayload)
# Validates token payload against schema using pytastic
```

Key rotation support:

```python
signer = SecureSerializer(
    ["new-secret", "old-secret"],  # Signs with first, verifies against all
    salt="auth"
)
```

## 20. Testing with Earth

Earth is heaven's built-in test client:

```python
import unittest
from heaven import App

class TestAPI(unittest.IsolatedAsyncioTestCase):
    async def asyncSetUp(self):
        self.app = App()
        # register routes...
        self.app.GET("/hello", hello_handler)

    async def test_hello(self):
        async with self.app.earth.test() as client:
            req, res, ctx = await client.GET("/hello")
            self.assertEqual(res.status, 200)

    async def test_create(self):
        async with self.app.earth.test() as client:
            req, res, ctx = await client.POST("/users",
                body={"name": "Ray", "email": "ray@example.com"}
            )
            self.assertEqual(res.status, 201)
            data = res.json
            self.assertEqual(data["name"], "Ray")
```

### Earth Methods

```python
async with app.earth.test() as client:
    req, res, ctx = await client.GET("/path")
    req, res, ctx = await client.POST("/path", body={...})
    req, res, ctx = await client.PUT("/path", body={...})
    req, res, ctx = await client.PATCH("/path", body={...})
    req, res, ctx = await client.DELETE("/path")
```

### File Uploads

```python
async with app.earth.test() as client:
    req, res, ctx = await client.upload("/upload",
        files={"avatar": ("photo.jpg", b"image-data")},
        data={"username": "ray"}
    )
```

### WebSocket Testing

```python
async with app.earth.test() as client:
    ws = client.SOCKET("/chat")
    await ws.connect()
    await ws.send("hello")
    msg = await ws.receive(timeout=2.0)
    await ws.close()
```

### Session Tracking

`test(track_session=True)` (default) automatically tracks cookies across requests:

```python
async with app.earth.test() as client:
    await client.POST("/login", body={"user": "admin"})
    # Session cookie is automatically sent on next request:
    req, res, ctx = await client.GET("/dashboard")
```

### Mocking Lifecycle Hooks

Register swaps BEFORE entering the test context manager, so they take effect during startup:

```python
async def real_startup(app):
    app.keep("db", await connect_to_database())

async def mock_startup(app):
    app.keep("db", FakeDB())

app.earth.swap(real_startup, mock_startup)
async with app.earth.test() as client:
    # mock_startup runs instead of real_startup
    req, res, ctx = await client.GET("/data")
```

### Bypassing Middleware in Tests

```python
app.earth.bypass(auth_middleware)
async with app.earth.test() as client:
    # auth_middleware is skipped during this test
    req, res, ctx = await client.GET("/protected")
```

### Atomic Test Factories

For unit-testing individual components without full request simulation:

```python
req = app.earth.request("/users/1", method="GET")
res = app.earth.response()
ctx = app.earth.context()

# Or all three at once:
req, res, ctx = app.earth.trio("/users/1", method="GET")

# Short aliases:
req = app.earth.req("/users/1")
res = app.earth.res()
ctx = app.earth.ctx()
```

## 21. Lookup Utility

Dot-notation access for dictionaries:

```python
from heaven.utils import Lookup

config = Lookup({"database": {"host": "localhost", "port": 5432}})
print(config.database.host)   # "localhost"
print(config.database.port)   # 5432
```

Also available via `app._`:

```python
app.keep("settings", {"theme": "dark"})
print(app._.settings.theme)  # "dark"
```

## 22. Imports & Error Classes

### Top-level imports from heaven:

```python
from heaven import App, Application, Router, Server
from heaven import Request, Response, Context, Key
from heaven import Pytastic, PytasticError, ValidationError
```

### Error classes:

```python
from heaven.errors import AbortException, SubdomainError, UrlError, UrlDuplicateError
```

- `AbortException` - raised internally when `res.abort()` is called
- `SubdomainError` - raised for subdomain configuration errors
- `UrlError` - raised when a route doesn't start with `/`
- `UrlDuplicateError` - raised when registering a duplicate route

### Security exceptions:

```python
from heaven.security import SecurityError, BadSignature, SignatureExpired
```

## 23. Full Application Example

Project structure:

```
myapp/
├── main.py
├── schemas.py
├── handlers/
│   └── todos.py
└── middleware/
    └── logging.py
```

```python
# schemas.py
from typing import TypedDict, Annotated

class CreateTodo(TypedDict):
    title: Annotated[str, "min_len=1; max_len=200"]
    done: bool

class Todo(TypedDict):
    id: int
    title: str
    done: bool
```

```python
# handlers/todos.py
from heaven import Key

db_key = Key[list]("todos")

async def list_todos(req, res, ctx):
    todos = req.app.peek(db_key)
    res.body = todos
    res.status = 200

async def create_todo(req, res, ctx):
    todos = req.app.peek(db_key)
    todo = {"id": len(todos) + 1, **req.data}
    todos.append(todo)
    res.body = todo
    res.status = 201
```

```python
# middleware/logging.py
async def log_request(req, res, ctx):
    print(f"{req.method} {req.url}")
```

```python
# main.py
from typing import List
from heaven import App, Key
from schemas import CreateTodo, Todo

app = App(protect_output=True)

db_key = Key[list]("todos")

# -- Lifecycle --
async def startup(app):
    app.keep(db_key, [])

app.ONCE(startup)

# -- Middleware --
app.BEFORE("/*", "middleware.logging.log_request")

# -- Schema + Routes --
app.schema.GET("/todos", returns=List[Todo], summary="List all todos")
app.GET("/todos", "handlers.todos.list_todos")

app.schema.POST("/todos", expects=CreateTodo, returns=Todo, summary="Create a todo")
app.POST("/todos", "handlers.todos.create_todo")

# -- Docs & CORS --
app.DOCS("/docs", title="Todo API", version="1.0.0")
app.cors(origins=["*"])
```
